outputs.L1:
The invention pertains to a method for preparing succinic acid, comprising the following steps:

—providing an aqueous magnesium succinate solution to an acidification step, wherein the magnesium succinate solution is acidified through the addition of hydrogen chloride, thereby yielding an aqueous solution comprising succinic acid and magnesium chloride;

—subjecting the aqueous solution comprising succinic acid and magnesium chloride obtained from the acidification step to a treatment step with activated carbon;

—precipitating succinic acid from the aqueous mixture comprising succinic acid and magnesium chloride resulting from the activated carbon treatment step, thereby forming solid succinic acid and a magnesium chloride solution;

—separating the solid succinic acid from the magnesium chloride solution;

—subjecting the magnesium chloride solution to thermal decomposition at a temperature of at least 300 °C, whereby the magnesium chloride is decomposed into magnesium oxide and hydrogen chloride; and

—recycling the hydrogen chloride generated in the thermal decomposition step back to the acidification step.

It has been found that the method according to the invention yields succinic acid crystals with superior properties compared to those produced by an analogous method in which no activated carbon treatment is employed.

outputs.L2:
This invention relates to a process for producing succinic acid that involves the following stages:

—An aqueous solution of magnesium succinate is introduced into an acidification stage, where hydrogen chloride is added to acidify it, yielding an aqueous solution that contains both succinic acid and magnesium chloride;

—The resulting aqueous solution containing succinic acid and magnesium chloride from the acidification stage undergoes treatment using activated carbon;

—Following the activated carbon treatment, succinic acid is precipitated out of the aqueous mixture of succinic acid and magnesium chloride during a precipitation stage, producing solid succinic acid alongside a magnesium chloride solution;

—The solid succinic acid is then separated away from the magnesium chloride solution;

—The magnesium chloride solution is heated to at least 300°C for thermal decomposition, which breaks down the magnesium chloride into magnesium oxide and hydrogen chloride;

—The hydrogen chloride produced during this thermal decomposition is then returned to the acidification stage for reuse.

Testing has demonstrated that this inventive process yields succinic acid crystals of superior quality compared to an equivalent process that omits the activated carbon treatment.
